export const RP1={title:'The event and what judges reward',hook:'Research Poster asks your team to act like real health researchers: ask a question that matters to your community, collect your own data, analyze it and share the findings on a professional 48-by-36-inch poster. New for 2026–27, it is a team event for two to four members. Understanding the rules and rubric from the start keeps your research on track.',objectives:['Describe the event’s rounds, display time and scoring.','Explain the requirement for original community research.','List the eight required poster components.','Plan a research timeline within the HOSA membership year.'],sections:[
['The event at a glance',[
'Research Poster is a **team event for 2–4 members** in the **secondary** and **postsecondary/collegiate** divisions. New for 2026–27, it moved to the **Teamwork** category, and teams have **five minutes** to present. Teams pose a **health-related research question** relevant to their community, **conduct their own research** and develop a poster showcasing the findings.',
'**Round One** is the pre-judged **digital poster**, uploaded by one member as **one PDF** by **May 15**, worth **95 points**. At ILC, every team attends the **Required Project Display Time** with its printed **48″ × 36″ landscape** poster (missing it costs **15 points** from Round Two). Top teams give a **five-minute presentation** standing beside their poster, worth **70 points**.',
'Top three winners in each division may have abstracts featured in the fall HOSA e-Magazine.',
"Because pre-judging happens before the conference and results are released after display time, every team should prepare fully for Round Two. Teams that advance will have little time between the announcement and their appointment.",
{t:'table',caption:'Event structure',head:['Part','What you do','Points'],rows:[['Round One','Pre-judged poster PDF','95'],['Display Time','Stand with your printed poster at ILC','−15 if missed'],['Round Two','5-minute presentation beside the poster','70'],['Total','Round One + Round Two','165']]}]],
['Original community research',[
'The goal is to **conduct your own research**, not summarize someone else’s. Your question must be researchable **in your community** using methods such as **surveys**, **interviews**, a **scientific study** or **observational ethnography**.',
'Topics must be **health-related**, with flexibility to choose something of local interest and importance. The guidelines’ examples include community strategies to reduce mental health stigma, combating postpartum depression in teen mothers and reducing juvenile incarceration through positive male role models.',
'Research must be conducted within the **current HOSA membership year (July 1, 2026–May 15, 2027)**.',
"Local research does not have to be large to be valuable. A well-designed survey of one school or observations in a few community settings can produce findings that matter to local leaders.",
sort('Original research or summary?',['Original research','Only a summary'],[['Surveying 150 students about sleep habits',0],['Rewriting a CDC report in your own words',1],['Interviewing local pharmacists about medication disposal',0],['Listing statistics from news articles',1],['Observing hand hygiene at a school cafeteria',0]])]],
['The eight poster components',[
'Every poster must include: **(a) Title**—highlights the research, includes competitors’ names, division, chapter number, school and chartered association (100 words maximum); **(b) Abstract** (about 250 words); **(c) Methods** (about 200 words); **(d) Results** (about 200 words); **(e) Conclusions** (about 200 words); **(f) References** in APA (about 100 words); **(g) Acknowledgements**; and **(h) Images**—2 to 5 graphs, tables, statistics, quotes, photos or illustrations.',
'The best posters are **self-contained and self-explanatory**—observers should understand the research without the team present.',
"The word counts are suggestions for most sections, but the title limit of 100 words is a firm maximum. Plan section lengths early so the layout has room for charts and images.",
"Treat the poster as the final product of the research, not the starting point. Strong posters come from strong data, so most of the year should go into planning, collecting and analyzing.",
flip('Poster components and limits',[['Title','≤100 words, with team details'],['Abstract','About 250 words'],['Methods','About 200 words'],['Results','About 200 words, with charts'],['Conclusions','About 200 words'],['Images','2 to 5']])]],
['How judges score',[
'**Round One (95):** the **research question** (10); **title**, **references** and **acknowledgement** (5 each); **abstract** and **methods** (10 each); **results**, **conclusions** and **images** (10 each); and **poster design**—artistic design and appearance and organization (10 each).',
'**Round Two (70):** **opening**, **presentation of the research** and **connection to the poster** (15 each); delivery—**voice**, **stage presence**, **diction** and **team participation** (5 each); and **poster size** (5).',
'The research question alone is worth 10 points—choose it carefully.',
"Print the rating sheets and keep them with your research notes. Checking each section against the descriptions for top scores helps the team focus effort where it earns the most points.",
match('Rubric item and round',[['Research question','Round One (10)'],['Methods','Round One (10)'],['Artistic design','Round One (10)'],['Opening (elevator pitch)','Round Two (15)'],['Connection to the poster','Round Two (15)'],['Poster size','Round Two (5)']])]],
['A research timeline',[
'**July–September:** form the team, choose a topic and question, review background literature and plan methods and consent. **October–January:** collect data. **February–March:** analyze data and write sections. **March–April:** design the poster, get feedback and revise. **By May 15:** upload one PDF. **May–June:** print the poster and rehearse the presentation.',
'Start data collection only after planning consent procedures, and remember that research must fall within the membership year.',
"Build in extra time for unexpected delays, such as waiting for school permission or low survey response. A cushion of several weeks before the upload deadline prevents rushed analysis.",
order('Research Poster timeline',['Choose topic and research question','Review background literature','Plan methods and consent','Collect data','Analyze data','Write sections and design poster','Upload PDF by May 15','Print poster and rehearse'])]],
['Team roles',[
'With two to four members, divide work: a **research lead** (literature and question), a **data lead** (collection and analysis), a **design lead** (poster layout and images) and a **communications lead** (abstract, presentation). Everyone should understand the full project, because all members must take an active role in Round Two.',
'Hold regular meetings with your advisor to check progress and ethical practices.',
"Keep a shared research log with dates, decisions and files. A clear log helps any member answer judges’ questions and makes writing the methods section much easier.",
chk('Why must every member understand the full project?',['All members must take an active role in Round Two','Only one member will present','Judges never ask questions','The poster explains everything'],'Team participation is scored.')]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Starting the project',{a:{text:'Your team plans to summarize national statistics on teen vaping. (Scenario invented for practice.) Does this meet the event’s goal?',choices:[['No—design your own local study, such as surveying students at your school','b','Correct.',true],['Yes, summaries are enough','b','The goal is your own research.',false]]},b:{text:'You collected survey data in May 2026. Can you use it?',choices:[['No—research must be conducted between July 1, 2026, and May 15, 2027','c','Correct.',true],['Yes, any date is fine','c','Data must fall within the membership year.',false]]},c:{text:'Your team plans a new local survey this fall.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Teams of 2–4 (secondary and postsecondary) conduct original, health-related community research; Round One is a pre-judged poster PDF (95 points, one PDF by May 15); ILC display time is required (−15 if missed); Round Two is a 5-minute presentation (70 points).','Research must be your own (surveys, interviews, scientific study or observational ethnography) and conducted July 1, 2026–May 15, 2027.','The poster includes title, abstract, methods, results, conclusions, references, acknowledgements and 2–5 images, is self-explanatory and is printed 48″ × 36″ landscape.'],terms:[['Research question','A focused question your study is designed to answer.'],['Self-explanatory poster','A poster viewers can understand without the presenters.'],['Observational ethnography','Studying people’s behavior by observing them in natural settings.']],mcqs:[q('How many members may be on a Research Poster team?','2–4','1','5–8','Exactly 6','New for 2026–27.'),q('What size is the printed poster?','48″ × 36″ landscape','24″ × 36″ portrait','36″ × 48″ tri-fold','8.5″ × 11″','Size.'),q('How many points is Round One worth?','95','70','165','100','Pre-judged.'),q('When must research be conducted?','July 1, 2026–May 15, 2027','Any time','Only in summer','Before July 2026','Membership year.'),q('How many images should the poster include?','2 to 5','None','At least 10','Exactly 1','Images.'),q('What is the penalty for missing display time?','15 points from Round Two','5 points','Disqualification','None','Rule.'),q('Which is original research?','Surveying local students','Summarizing a CDC report','Copying news statistics','Rewriting a textbook','Own research.'),q('How long is the Round Two presentation?','5 minutes','10 minutes','3 minutes','15 minutes','New for 2026–27.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain the Research Poster event and how your team would plan an original community research project.',rubric:[['Event',['2-4','95','70','pdf','may 15','display time','5 minutes']],['Original research',['own research','community','health-related','survey','interview','membership year']],['Components',['title','abstract','methods','results','conclusions','references','acknowledgements','images']],['Planning',['timeline','consent','roles','analyze','design','print']]],model:'Research Poster is now a team event for two to four secondary or postsecondary members who pose a health-related research question relevant to their community, conduct their own research and present the findings on a poster. In Round One, judges score a pre-judged PDF of our poster, uploaded by one member by May 15, for 95 points; at ILC we must attend the Required Project Display Time with our printed 48-by-36-inch landscape poster or lose 15 points, and top teams give a five-minute presentation beside the poster worth 70 points. Our research must be original—not a summary of others’ work—using methods such as surveys, interviews, a scientific study or observational ethnography, and it must be conducted between July 1, 2026, and May 15, 2027. The poster must include a title with our names, division, chapter number, school and chartered association; an abstract of about 250 words; methods, results and conclusions of about 200 words each; APA references; acknowledgements; and two to five images such as graphs, tables and quotes, and it should be self-explanatory. We would plan a timeline: choose our question and review literature from July to September, plan methods and informed consent, collect data from October to January, analyze and write in February and March, design and revise the poster in March and April, upload by May 15 and then print and rehearse. We would assign research, data, design and communications leads while making sure every member understands the whole project.'},sources:[RULES,CTB]};

export const RP2={title:'Choosing a research question',hook:'A strong research question is focused, answerable and important—and it is worth 10 points on its own. “Teen health” is a topic; “How many hours of sleep do students at our high school get on school nights, and is sleep related to self-reported stress?” is a research question. This lesson helps your team move from a broad interest to a question you can actually answer with your own data.',objectives:['Identify health issues of local importance.','Narrow a topic into a focused research question.','Evaluate questions with the FINER criteria.','Write clear, measurable research questions and hypotheses.'],sections:[
['Finding a local health issue',[
'Look for health issues that matter in **your community**: local data from your health department or county health rankings, problems people talk about at school, in clinics or in the news, and needs identified by community organizations. Examples include sleep and stress among students, vaping, food insecurity, access to mental health care, sports injuries, sun safety, medication disposal or physical activity.',
'Talk with teachers, school nurses, coaches, health professionals and community leaders—they often know which questions need answers.',
'Choose a topic your team cares about; you will spend months with it.',
"County Health Rankings and local health department reports can point you toward issues where your community differs from state or national averages, which often makes a strong, relevant research topic.",
sort('Health-related topic?',['Health-related','Not health-related'],[['Sleep and stress among students',0],['Favorite sports teams at school',1],['Access to free menstrual products',0],['Cafeteria seating preferences unrelated to health',1],['Knowledge of safe medication disposal',0],['Hand hygiene before lunch',0]])]],
['From topic to question',[
'Narrow a broad **topic** into a focused **question** by specifying the **population** (who), the **variable(s)** (what you will measure), the **setting** (where) and sometimes a **comparison** or **relationship**. For example: topic—sleep; narrower—sleep among students; question—“What percentage of students at our high school sleep fewer than eight hours on school nights, and is shorter sleep associated with higher self-reported stress?”',
'Good questions usually start with **what**, **how many**, **how often**, **to what extent** or **is there a relationship between**.',
"Write several versions of your question and compare them side by side. Small changes in wording—such as “how often” versus “whether”—can change what data you need to collect.",
order('Narrowing a topic',['Broad topic: teen health','Narrower: sleep among teens','Population: students at our high school','Variables: hours of sleep and stress level','Research question: Is shorter sleep associated with higher stress?'])]],
['The FINER criteria',[
'Researchers often check questions with **FINER**: **Feasible** (can you collect enough data with your time, skills and resources?), **Interesting** (to you and your audience), **Novel** (adds something new, at least locally), **Ethical** (can be studied without harming participants) and **Relevant** (matters to your community and health).',
'A question that fails any criterion should be revised. For example, a question requiring medical records or invasive testing is neither feasible nor appropriate for a student team.',
"Feasibility includes access. If you cannot reach enough participants in your chosen group, such as teen parents or health workers, consider adjusting the population or method early.",
match('FINER letter and meaning',[['F','Feasible with available time and resources'],['I','Interesting to researchers and audience'],['N','Novel, adding new information'],['E','Ethical, without harm to participants'],['R','Relevant to community health'],['Failing a criterion','Revise the question']])]],
['Hypotheses and variables',[
'A **hypothesis** predicts the answer: “Students who sleep fewer than seven hours will report higher stress than students who sleep eight or more hours.” Not every study needs a hypothesis—descriptive questions (“What percentage…?”) may not—but relationship questions benefit from one.',
'Identify **variables**: the **independent variable** (the factor you think influences the outcome, such as sleep hours) and the **dependent variable** (the outcome, such as stress level). Also note **confounding variables** that could affect results, such as workload or sports schedules.',
"Write your hypothesis before collecting data and do not change it afterward to match results. Reporting whether your prediction was supported is part of honest research.",
"Descriptive studies are still valuable. Knowing how common a behavior or belief is in your community can guide local programs even without testing a relationship.",
sort('Independent or dependent variable?',['Independent (influencing factor)','Dependent (outcome)'],[['Hours of sleep',0],['Self-reported stress level',1],['Attending a hand-washing lesson',0],['Hand-washing frequency afterward',1],['Daily screen time',0],['Reported headaches',1]])]],
['Writing a clear question',[
'A clear research question is **specific**, **measurable**, **answerable with your data** and **neutral** (not assuming the answer). Avoid questions that are too broad (“How can we fix teen mental health?”), unanswerable with your methods or loaded (“Why are students so lazy about exercise?”).',
'Write the question at the top of your planning documents and on the poster, and use it to guide every decision about methods and analysis.',
"Share the draft question with your advisor and a professional in the field. Their feedback can reveal whether the question is clear, realistic and meaningful.",
ex('Improving a research question','Revise a broad question.',['Original: “Is vaping bad?”','Problem: too broad, already known, no population or measure','Revision: “What do students at our school believe about the nicotine content of vapes, and how accurate are their beliefs?”','Check: feasible survey, local, measurable, ethical, relevant'],'A focused, answerable question about local knowledge.')]],
['Linking the question to methods',[
'Your question determines your methods. **Descriptive** questions (how many, what percentage) often use **surveys** or **observation**. **Relationship** questions (is X associated with Y) need data on both variables from the same participants. **Experience** questions (how do people feel about…) suit **interviews** or **focus groups**. **Intervention** questions (does a lesson change behavior?) may use **pre- and post-measures**.',
'Choose a question your team can answer within the membership year with your available participants.',
"Keep the question visible during every stage. When deciding whether to add a survey item or chart, ask whether it helps answer the research question; if not, leave it out.",
chk('Which method best fits “How do teen mothers in our community describe barriers to mental health care?”',['Interviews','A sugar test','Counting cars','A taste test'],'Experiences suit interviews.')]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Question workshop',{a:{text:'Your team’s question is “How can we end obesity?” (Scenario invented for practice.) What is the problem?',choices:[['It is too broad and not answerable with your own data—narrow it','b','Correct.',true],['It is perfect','b','Use FINER to narrow it.',false]]},b:{text:'You revise it to “What percentage of students at our school meet the daily physical activity recommendation, and what barriers do they report?” Is it FINER?',choices:[['Yes—feasible survey, interesting, locally novel, ethical and relevant','c','Correct.',true],['No—it is unethical','c','A voluntary anonymous survey is ethical.',false]]},c:{text:'Your question is ready for background research.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Find local, health-related issues through community data and conversations with teachers, nurses and health professionals; choose a topic your team cares about.','Narrow topics by population, variables, setting and relationships; check questions with FINER (feasible, interesting, novel, ethical, relevant); write hypotheses for relationship questions.','Identify independent, dependent and confounding variables; write specific, measurable, answerable and neutral questions; choose methods that fit the question (surveys, observation, interviews, pre/post).'],terms:[['Hypothesis','A testable prediction about the answer to a research question.'],['Independent variable','The factor thought to influence an outcome.'],['Dependent variable','The outcome being measured.']],mcqs:[q('How many points is the research question worth in Round One?','10','5','15','20','Rubric.'),q('What does the F in FINER stand for?','Feasible','Famous','Fast','Formal','Criteria.'),q('Which is a focused research question?','What percentage of students at our school sleep under 8 hours?','Is sleep good?','How do we fix health?','Why is everyone tired?','Specific.'),q('Which is a dependent variable in a sleep and stress study?','Stress level','Hours of sleep','Bedtime','Screen time','Outcome.'),q('A confounding variable is:','A factor that could affect results besides the variable studied','The main outcome','The research question','The poster title','Bias.'),q('Which question is loaded?','“Why are students so lazy about exercise?”','“How many students exercise daily?”','“What barriers do students report?”','“How often do students walk to school?”','Neutral wording.'),q('Experience questions are best answered with:','Interviews','Weighing objects','Counting cars','Blood tests','Methods.'),q('Which fails the E in FINER?','A study requiring invasive testing of classmates','An anonymous survey','Observing hand washing','Interviewing coaches','Ethical.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe how your team would develop a strong research question for Research Poster.',rubric:[['Local issue',['community','local','data','health-related','nurse','teachers']],['Narrowing',['population','variables','setting','relationship','specific','measurable']],['FINER',['feasible','interesting','novel','ethical','relevant']],['Hypothesis and methods',['hypothesis','independent','dependent','confounding','survey','interviews']]],model:'We would start by looking for a health issue that matters locally, reviewing our county health rankings and talking with our school nurse, health teacher and coaches. If they told us students often seem exhausted and stressed, we would narrow the broad topic of teen health to sleep among students at our high school, then specify our variables—hours of sleep on school nights and self-reported stress—to create the question: “What percentage of students at our high school sleep fewer than eight hours on school nights, and is shorter sleep associated with higher self-reported stress?” We would check it with the FINER criteria: it is feasible with an anonymous survey during the school year, interesting to students and families, novel because no local data exist, ethical because participation is voluntary and anonymous, and relevant to student health. Because it asks about a relationship, we would write a hypothesis: students who sleep fewer than seven hours will report higher stress than those who sleep eight or more. Sleep hours would be our independent variable, stress our dependent variable, and we would note possible confounding variables such as homework load, jobs and sports schedules, asking about them in our survey. We would keep the question neutral and measurable, avoiding broad or loaded wording, and choose a survey method because it can collect both variables from the same students.'},sources:[RULES,CTB]};

export const RP3={title:'Background research and references',hook:'Before collecting data, real researchers find out what is already known. Background research shows why your question matters, helps you choose methods and gives you something to compare your results with. It also produces the references section of your poster. Good background research makes your own study smarter and more credible.',objectives:['Find credible background sources for a health topic.','Summarize what is known and identify gaps.','Use background research to shape methods and comparisons.','Cite sources in APA style within the poster’s limits.'],sections:[
['Why background research matters',[
'Background research helps you **justify** your question (why it matters), **avoid repeating** work that is already well established, **choose methods** that others have used successfully (such as validated survey questions) and **compare** your results with national or published data.',
'On the poster, background appears mainly in the **abstract** (purpose and problem) and **conclusions** (how your results fit what is known). References are listed in APA style.',
'Your study should add something—at least local information—to what is known.',
"Background research also helps you anticipate judges’ questions. Knowing what national studies have found lets you explain whether your local results are similar or different and why.",
match('Purpose of background research and example',[['Justify the question','National data show many teens sleep too little'],['Avoid repetition','A local question no one has studied'],['Choose methods','Use survey questions from a published study'],['Compare results','Our school versus national survey results'],['Interpret findings','Explain results using known research'],['Identify gaps','No data on our community’s students']])]],
['Finding credible sources',[
'Search **government health agencies** (CDC, NIH, MedlinePlus, state and local health departments), **national surveys** (such as the Youth Risk Behavior Survey), **peer-reviewed journals** through **PubMed** or **Google Scholar**, **professional organizations** and **reputable university** sources.',
'Use specific keywords and synonyms (“adolescent sleep,” “teen sleep duration,” “high school stress”) and filter for recent publications. Read **abstracts** first to decide which articles are useful.',
'School and public libraries often provide access to journal databases.',
"Check the publication date of every source. Health guidance and statistics change over time, and recent sources are usually more relevant to current conditions.",
"Government reports often include summary tables you can use for comparison. Note the year and population of each table so comparisons with your data are fair.",
sort('Credible background source?',['Credible','Use with caution'],[['A CDC data brief on adolescent sleep',0],['A sleep-supplement company blog',1],['A peer-reviewed study found on PubMed',0],['An anonymous social media post',1],['A state health department report',0],['A website with no author or date',1]])]],
['Reading research articles',[
'Research articles usually include an **abstract**, **introduction** (background and purpose), **methods**, **results**, **discussion** and **references**—the same structure as your poster. Focus on the abstract, the methods (who was studied and how) and the main results and conclusions.',
'Note each study’s **population**, **sample size**, **methods** and **limitations**. A study of adults in another country may not apply directly to teens in your community.',
'Record key facts and full citation information as you read.',
"Pay special attention to how other researchers measured variables. If a published study asked about sleep in a specific way, using similar wording makes your results easier to compare.",
order('Reading an article efficiently',['Read the title and abstract','Skim the introduction for background','Read the methods: who and how','Study the main results and figures','Read the discussion and limitations','Record key facts and citation details'])]],
['Summarizing what is known',[
'Organize notes by **theme** rather than by article: what is known about the problem’s size, causes, effects and solutions. Identify the **gap** your study fills—often the lack of local data or of information about a specific group.',
'Write a short background summary (a few sentences) for your abstract and introduction, citing sources. Keep your own voice; paraphrase and cite rather than copying.',
"A simple source table with columns for the citation, population, method, key finding and relevance to your study helps the team see patterns across sources quickly.",
"If background research shows your question has already been thoroughly studied locally, consider a related question that adds something new, such as a different group or an intervention.",
ex('A background summary','Draft background sentences for a sleep study (sources invented for practice).',['Problem: national surveys show many high school students sleep fewer than eight hours on school nights','Effects: short sleep is linked with poorer mood, attention and safety','Gap: no data exist for students at our school','Purpose: our study measures sleep and stress locally'],'A concise, cited background that leads directly to the research question (sources invented for practice).')]],
['APA references',[
'The poster’s **references** section lists literature that guided the project in **APA** style (about 100 words suggested). Since space is limited, include your **most important** sources. A basic APA journal reference: Author, A. A., & Author, B. B. (Year). Title of article. *Journal Name, volume*(issue), pages. https://doi.org/xxxx',
'A web page: Organization. (Year, Month Day). *Title of page*. Site name. URL. The reference item is worth 5 points—at least one reference must be included.',
"Use a citation manager or a shared document to record references as you read. Formatting citations at the end is much harder when details such as page numbers or DOIs are missing.",
chk('About how many words are suggested for the references section?',['About 100','About 1,000','About 10','None—references are not included'],'Suggested limit.')]],
['Avoiding plagiarism',[
'**Plagiarism** is presenting others’ words or ideas as your own. Avoid it by **paraphrasing** in your own words, **quoting** sparingly with quotation marks and **citing** every source. Your data, analysis and conclusions must be your team’s own.',
'If you use AI tools to help brainstorm or search, verify every fact in an original source and follow HOSA’s rules on original work.',
"When paraphrasing, read the original, close it and write the idea in your own words, then compare to make sure the meaning is accurate and the wording is different.",
sort('Acceptable practice?',['Acceptable','Plagiarism'],[['Paraphrasing a study and citing it',0],['Copying a paragraph without quotation marks',1],['Quoting a short phrase with citation',0],['Using another study’s results as your own',1],['Citing a CDC statistic in your abstract',0]])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Background research',{a:{text:'Your team finds a national study showing teens sleep too little. (Scenario invented for practice.) Should you change your question?',choices:[['No—use it as background and compare it with your local data','b','Correct.',true],['Yes—the topic is already done','b','Local data add new information.',false]]},b:{text:'You find a validated sleep survey question in a published study. Can you use it?',choices:[['Yes, with citation, so your results can be compared','c','Correct.',true],['No, never reuse questions','c','Citing validated questions strengthens methods.',false]]},c:{text:'Your background research strengthens your methods and comparisons.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Background research justifies the question, avoids repetition, guides methods (such as validated survey items) and provides comparisons; your study should add new, at least local, information.','Use credible sources (CDC, NIH, national surveys, PubMed, state and local health departments); read abstracts, methods, results and limitations; organize notes by theme and identify the gap.','List the most important sources in APA style (about 100 words; at least one reference is required); paraphrase, quote sparingly, cite every source and keep data and conclusions your own.'],terms:[['Literature review','A summary of existing research on a topic.'],['Gap','Missing knowledge that a new study can address.'],['Plagiarism','Presenting someone else’s words or ideas as your own.']],mcqs:[q('Why do background research?','To justify the question and guide methods','To copy results','To skip data collection','To fill the poster','Purpose.'),q('Which database finds peer-reviewed health research?','PubMed','A shopping site','A video game','A sports app','Database.'),q('Which section of an article tells you who was studied and how?','Methods','Title','References','Acknowledgements','Structure.'),q('Notes should be organized by:','Theme','Font size','Color','Alphabetical author only','Synthesis.'),q('What style is preferred for references?','APA','No style','Emoji','Random','Health science.'),q('About how many words are suggested for references?','About 100','About 500','About 10','About 1,000','Limit.'),q('Which is plagiarism?','Copying a paragraph without quotation marks','Paraphrasing with citation','Quoting with citation','Citing a statistic','Integrity.'),q('A gap in research is:','Missing knowledge your study can address','A blank space on the poster','A spelling error','A type of chart','Definition.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how your team would conduct background research and use it in your Research Poster project.',rubric:[['Purpose',['justify','methods','compare','gap','local']],['Sources',['cdc','nih','pubmed','peer-reviewed','national survey','health department']],['Reading and summarizing',['abstract','methods','limitations','theme','summary','paraphrase']],['References',['apa','100 words','cite','plagiarism','quotation']]],model:'Before collecting data on sleep and stress at our school, we would search for what is already known. We would review CDC data briefs and the Youth Risk Behavior Survey on how much high school students sleep, NIH and MedlinePlus information on the effects of short sleep and peer-reviewed studies found through PubMed using keywords such as adolescent sleep duration and high school stress. For each article, we would read the abstract first, then the methods to see who was studied and how, the main results and the limitations, recording key facts and full citations. We would organize our notes by theme—how common short sleep is, its effects on mood and attention and strategies that help—and identify the gap: no data exist for students at our school. This background would justify our question, help us choose validated survey questions from published studies so our results can be compared with national data and help us interpret our findings in the conclusions. On the poster, a few cited sentences in the abstract would explain the problem and purpose, and our references section would list our most important sources in APA style within about 100 words. We would paraphrase in our own words, quote sparingly with quotation marks, cite every source and verify any fact found with AI tools in an original source, keeping our data, analysis and conclusions entirely our own.'},sources:[RULES,PUBMED,APA]};

export const RP4={title:'Research design and methods',hook:'Methods are the recipe for your research: who you study, how you collect data and how you analyze it. Judges award full points when methods are explicitly explained, including the target population, accurate data collection, analysis and possible errors and biases. Careful design is what makes your results believable.',objectives:['Choose methods that fit the research question.','Define the target population and select a sample.','Design surveys, interviews and observations carefully.','Identify sources of error and bias.'],sections:[
['Research methods',[
'The guidelines list **surveys**, **interviews**, **scientific studies** and **observational ethnography**. **Surveys** collect standardized answers from many people—good for “how many” and relationship questions. **Interviews** and **focus groups** explore experiences and opinions in depth. **Scientific studies** test something under controlled conditions, such as measuring bacteria on surfaces before and after cleaning. **Observational ethnography** studies behavior in natural settings, such as observing hand washing in a cafeteria.',
'**Mixed methods** combine approaches—for example, a survey plus a few interviews—to provide both numbers and stories.',
"Each method has strengths and limits. Surveys reach many people but may lack depth; interviews provide depth but involve fewer people. Explaining why you chose your method shows judges thoughtful design.",
match('Method and best use',[['Survey','Measuring how common a behavior or belief is'],['Interview','Exploring experiences in depth'],['Focus group','Group discussion of opinions'],['Scientific study','Testing under controlled conditions'],['Observational ethnography','Watching behavior in natural settings'],['Mixed methods','Combining numbers and stories']])]],
['Population and sampling',[
'The **target population** is the group you want to learn about, such as all students at your high school. A **sample** is the group you actually study. **Random sampling** gives everyone an equal chance to be chosen and reduces bias. **Convenience sampling** (whoever is available) is easier but may not represent the population.',
'Larger samples generally give more reliable results. Report your **sample size** and **response rate** (respondents ÷ people invited).',
"Stratified sampling—selecting participants from each subgroup, such as every grade level—helps ensure that important groups are represented in proportion to the population.",
"Consider practical limits on sample size, such as how many homerooms will participate and how long data collection can run. A realistic plan is better than an ambitious one that cannot be completed.",
ex('Response rate','You invited 400 students and 172 completed the survey (invented for practice). What is the response rate?',['Response rate = respondents ÷ invited','172 ÷ 400 = 0.43','Convert to a percentage: 43%'],'43% response rate (numbers invented for practice).')]],
['Designing a survey',[
'Good survey questions are **clear**, **short**, **neutral** and ask about **one thing at a time**. Use **closed-ended** questions (multiple choice, yes/no, rating scales) for easy analysis and a few **open-ended** questions for richer detail. Avoid **leading** questions (“Don’t you agree that…?”) and **double-barreled** questions (“Do you eat breakfast and lunch every day?”).',
'**Pilot test** the survey with a few people from the population to find confusing items, then revise. Keep it short—5 to 10 minutes—to improve completion.',
"Order survey questions logically, starting with easy, non-sensitive items and placing more personal questions later. Clear instructions at the top tell participants what to expect.",
"Include a few demographic questions, such as grade level, to describe your sample and compare subgroups, but ask only what you need for your analysis.",
sort('Good or poor survey question?',['Good','Poor'],[['“On school nights, how many hours do you usually sleep?”',0],['“Don’t you agree that phones ruin sleep?”',1],['“Rate your stress this week from 1 to 5.”',0],['“Do you exercise and eat vegetables daily?”',1],['“What is one barrier to getting more sleep?”',0]])]],
['Interviews and observations',[
'For **interviews**, prepare an **interview guide** with open-ended questions, ask the same core questions of each participant, record (with permission) or take careful notes and summarize responses into **themes**.',
'For **observations**, define exactly what you will record (for example, “washes hands with soap for at least 20 seconds”), use a **checklist** or tally sheet, observe at consistent times and have two observers compare results to improve **reliability**.',
'Clear definitions make data consistent and comparable.',
"For scientific studies, such as testing bacteria on surfaces, keep conditions consistent and use a control for comparison. Record procedures carefully so they can be repeated.",
order('Planning an observation study',['Define the behavior precisely','Create a tally sheet or checklist','Choose consistent times and places','Train two observers','Observe and record','Compare observers’ results'])]],
['Data collection accuracy',[
'Explain **how data were collected accurately**: consistent procedures, clear instructions, trained team members, standardized forms, anonymous responses for sensitive topics and careful data entry (double-checking a sample of entries).',
'Record the **dates**, **locations** and **number of participants** for the methods section. Store data securely.',
"Assign one team member to manage data collection materials and another to check completed forms. Dividing responsibilities reduces mistakes and missing information.",
"Write down your data collection procedure step by step before starting, so every team member follows the same process and the methods section can describe it accurately.",
chk('Which improves data accuracy?',['Using the same instructions and form for every participant','Changing questions halfway through','Letting friends answer for each other','Guessing missing answers'],'Consistency.')]],
['Errors and biases',[
'The rubric asks you to **explain possible errors and biases**. **Selection bias** occurs when the sample is not representative (only surveying students in one class). **Response bias** occurs when people answer in socially desirable ways (underreporting vaping). **Recall bias** affects memory-based answers. **Measurement error** comes from unclear questions or inconsistent observation. **Nonresponse bias** occurs if those who do not respond differ from those who do.',
'Describe how you reduced each bias (random sampling, anonymous surveys, clear definitions) and how remaining biases might affect results. Honest discussion strengthens your credibility.',
"Admitting limitations does not weaken your poster. Judges expect student research to have limits, and explaining them clearly shows mature scientific thinking.",
sort('Type of bias',['Selection bias','Response bias','Recall bias'],[['Surveying only the honors class',0],['Students underreporting vaping',1],['Estimating sleep from last month',2],['Only athletes respond to a fitness survey',0],['Overreporting exercise to look good',1],['Remembering meals from weeks ago',2]])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Methods review',{a:{text:'Your team surveyed only friends in your health science class. (Scenario invented for practice.) What is the main problem?',choices:[['Selection bias—the sample may not represent all students','b','Correct.',true],['No problem','b','Convenience samples can be biased.',false]]},b:{text:'How could you reduce this bias next time?',choices:[['Randomly select homerooms across all grades to invite','c','Correct.',true],['Survey only seniors','c','That would add a new bias.',false]]},c:{text:'You explain the limitation honestly on your poster.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Methods include surveys, interviews and focus groups, scientific studies and observational ethnography, or mixed methods; choose what fits the question.','Define the target population; random sampling reduces bias; report sample size and response rate; design clear, neutral, single-topic survey questions, pilot test them and use interview guides or observation checklists with clear definitions.','Explain accurate data collection and possible errors and biases—selection, response, recall, measurement and nonresponse—and how you reduced them.'],terms:[['Target population','The whole group a study aims to learn about.'],['Random sampling','Selecting participants so each has an equal chance.'],['Selection bias','Error from a sample that does not represent the population.']],mcqs:[q('Which method best measures how common a behavior is?','Survey','Single interview','Poster design','Abstract','Methods.'),q('Random sampling helps:','Reduce bias','Increase bias','Avoid consent','Shorten the poster','Sampling.'),q('If 150 of 300 invited students respond, the response rate is:','50%','150%','30%','15%','Calculation.'),q('Which is a leading question?','“Don’t you agree phones ruin sleep?”','“How many hours do you sleep?”','“Rate your stress 1–5.”','“What helps you sleep?”','Neutral wording.'),q('A double-barreled question:','Asks about two things at once','Is very short','Is open-ended','Is neutral','Clarity.'),q('Students underreporting vaping is an example of:','Response bias','Selection bias','Random sampling','Reliability','Bias.'),q('Why use two observers?','To improve reliability','To double the data falsely','To skip consent','To save time','Consistency.'),q('Which should the methods section explain?','Possible errors and biases','The team’s favorite colors','Judges’ names','Printing costs','Rubric.')],frq:{prompt:'Describe the research design and methods your team would use for a local health study, including sampling, tools and possible biases.',rubric:[['Method choice',['survey','interview','observation','scientific','mixed','fits']],['Population and sample',['target population','sample','random','sample size','response rate']],['Tools and accuracy',['clear','neutral','pilot','checklist','consistent','anonymous']],['Errors and bias',['selection','response','recall','measurement','nonresponse','reduce']]],model:'To study whether shorter sleep is associated with higher stress at our high school, we would use mixed methods: an anonymous survey and a few follow-up interviews. Our target population would be all students at our school, and to reduce selection bias we would randomly select homerooms from every grade and invite all students in them, reporting our sample size and response rate—for example, 172 of 400 invited students, or 43%. Our survey would include clear, neutral, single-topic questions, such as “On school nights, how many hours do you usually sleep?” and a 1-to-5 stress rating, adapted from validated questions in published studies, plus questions on homework, jobs and sports to account for confounding variables and one open-ended question about barriers to sleep. We would pilot test it with five students and revise confusing items. For interviews, we would use an interview guide with the same open-ended questions for six volunteers and summarize responses into themes. To collect data accurately, we would use the same instructions in every homeroom, keep responses anonymous and double-check data entry. In our methods section, we would explain possible errors and biases: selection bias if some homerooms declined, response bias if students exaggerated or understated sleep or stress, recall bias in estimating usual sleep and nonresponse bias if stressed students were less likely to respond, along with the steps we took to reduce each.'},sources:[RULES,STATS,CTB]};

export const RP5={title:'Research ethics and informed consent',hook:'Research involving people carries real responsibility. The guidelines make each team responsible for obtaining informed consent from participants and following ethical research practices, even though formal IRB approval is not required. Ethical research protects participants, builds trust and makes your findings credible.',objectives:['Apply the Belmont principles to student research.','Obtain informed consent and assent appropriately.','Protect privacy and confidentiality.','Identify and avoid ethical risks in health research.'],sections:[
['Ethical principles',[
'The **Belmont Report** describes three core principles for research with people. **Respect for persons** means treating participants as autonomous and protecting those with reduced autonomy, such as minors—this is the basis of **informed consent**. **Beneficence** means maximizing benefits and minimizing harms. **Justice** means fairly distributing the benefits and burdens of research.',
'These principles apply to your HOSA project, even without formal review by an **Institutional Review Board (IRB)**.',
"Ethical thinking should start at the planning stage, not after data are collected. Review each question and procedure and ask how it could affect participants.",
match('Belmont principle and application',[['Respect for persons','Voluntary, informed participation'],['Beneficence','Minimize risks such as embarrassment'],['Justice','Fair selection of participants'],['Protecting minors','Parental permission and student assent'],['Confidentiality','No names on survey responses'],['Voluntary participation','Students may skip or stop anytime']])]],
['Informed consent',[
'**Informed consent** means participants understand the study and agree voluntarily. Explain the **purpose**, **what participation involves** and how long it takes, **risks and benefits**, how **privacy** will be protected, that participation is **voluntary** and they may **stop at any time** without penalty and **who to contact** with questions.',
'For participants **under 18**, follow your school’s policies, which may require **parental or guardian permission** and the student’s own **assent**. Document consent appropriately—a signed form, a consent statement at the start of an online survey or verbal consent recorded in notes for interviews.',
"Use plain language in consent materials so participants of every reading level understand. Avoid jargon such as “confidentiality protocol” without explaining what it means.",
"For interviews with adults, such as health workers, explain the purpose, how quotes will be used and whether their names or job titles will appear, and get their agreement.",
order('Informed consent process',['Explain the purpose and procedures','Describe risks and benefits','Explain privacy protections','State that participation is voluntary','Answer questions','Obtain and document consent'])]],
['Privacy and confidentiality',[
'**Anonymous** data contain no identifying information at all. **Confidential** data may be linked to identities but are kept private by the researchers. For sensitive topics such as mental health, substance use or sexual health, anonymous surveys are usually best.',
'Collect only the information you need, store data securely (password-protected files), share results only in **aggregate** (group totals) and never reveal individuals’ answers. Remove names and identifying details from quotes.',
"If you use online survey tools, check settings to make sure they do not automatically collect email addresses or other identifying information unless you need it and have permission.",
"Destroy or securely archive identifiable information, such as consent forms with names, separately from survey data so responses cannot be linked to individuals.",
sort('Protects privacy?',['Protects privacy','Risks privacy'],[['Anonymous online survey with no names',0],['Posting individual responses in a group chat',1],['Reporting results as percentages',0],['Including a student’s name with a quote',1],['Storing data in a password-protected file',0],['Asking for home addresses without need',1]])]],
['Minimizing risk and harm',[
'Even simple surveys can cause **discomfort** if they ask about sensitive experiences. Minimize risk: avoid unnecessary personal questions, allow participants to **skip** questions, provide **resources** (such as a counselor or crisis line) when topics touch on mental health and never pressure anyone to participate.',
'Student researchers should **not** perform medical procedures, collect blood or body fluids, give medications or diagnose anyone. Scientific studies should use safe materials and supervision.',
"Plan what to do if a participant shares something concerning, such as a risk to their safety. Discuss with your advisor in advance which adult to inform under school policy.",
"Avoid offering rewards large enough to pressure people into participating. Small thank-you gestures are acceptable if your school allows them and they do not affect voluntary choice.",
chk('Your survey asks about stress and feelings of sadness. What should you include?',['Information about school counseling and crisis resources','Participants’ names','A requirement to answer every question','A grade for participating'],'Minimize harm and provide support.')]],
['Working with schools and organizations',[
'Get **permission** from your school administration or community organization before recruiting participants or collecting data on their premises. Many schools require approval for surveys of students. Share your survey and consent forms with your advisor and the school before starting.',
'If you work with a community agency, clarify how data will be used and shared, and thank them in your **acknowledgements**.',
"Keep copies of permission letters, consent forms and your survey in your research log. They show judges that your team followed ethical practices if asked.",
ex('A consent statement','Write the opening of an anonymous student survey (details invented for practice).',['Purpose: “Our HOSA team is studying sleep and stress among students at our school.”','Procedures: “This anonymous survey takes about 5 minutes.”','Voluntary: “You may skip any question or stop at any time.”','Privacy: “We will not collect names; results will be reported as group totals.”','Support: “If you feel stressed, contact the school counselor.”','Consent: “Continuing means you agree to take part.”'],'A clear, ethical consent statement (details invented for practice).')]],
['Honesty and integrity',[
'Research integrity means **never fabricating** (making up) data, **never falsifying** (changing) data to fit expectations and **never plagiarizing**. Report results honestly, even if they do not support your hypothesis. Document your procedures so others could repeat your study.',
'Keep raw data and records through the competition in case questions arise.',
"Mention your consent and privacy procedures briefly in the methods section of the poster. A single sentence such as “Participation was voluntary and anonymous” reassures readers.",
sort('Ethical research practice?',['Ethical','Unethical'],[['Reporting results that contradict the hypothesis',0],['Adding fake responses to increase sample size',1],['Documenting procedures carefully',0],['Removing data that do not fit the expected result',1],['Crediting others’ ideas',0]])]],
['Practice scenario',[
kase('Ethics check',{a:{text:'Your team wants to survey students about vaping during class without telling them the purpose. (Scenario invented for practice.) What should you do?',choices:[['Get school permission, explain the purpose and keep participation voluntary and anonymous','b','Correct.',true],['Collect the data quickly without explanation','b','Informed consent is required.',false]]},b:{text:'Your results do not support your hypothesis. What do you report?',choices:[['The actual results, honestly, with possible explanations','c','Correct.',true],['Change some numbers','c','Falsification is unethical.',false]]},c:{text:'Your research is ethical and credible.',end:true}})]]
],takeaways:['Belmont principles: respect for persons (informed consent; protect minors), beneficence (maximize benefits, minimize harms) and justice (fair participant selection); IRB approval is not required, but ethical practice is.','Informed consent explains purpose, procedures, risks, benefits, privacy, voluntary participation and contacts; follow school policies for minors (parental permission and assent); document consent.','Protect privacy (anonymous or confidential data, aggregate reporting, secure storage), minimize risk (skip options, resources, no medical procedures), get school permission and never fabricate, falsify or plagiarize.'],terms:[['Informed consent','Voluntary agreement after understanding a study’s purpose, procedures and risks.'],['Anonymous data','Data with no information that could identify participants.'],['Fabrication','Making up research data.']],mcqs:[q('Is formal IRB approval required for HOSA Research Poster?','No, but ethical practices are expected','Yes, always','Only for interviews','Only for postsecondary','Rule.'),q('Which Belmont principle underlies informed consent?','Respect for persons','Justice','Beneficence','Efficiency','Principle.'),q('Participants under 18 may need:','Parental permission and their own assent','No consent','Only judges’ approval','Payment','Minors.'),q('Anonymous data contain:','No identifying information','Names and addresses','Phone numbers','Photos of participants','Privacy.'),q('Results should usually be reported:','In aggregate (group totals)','With individual names','As screenshots of answers','Not at all','Confidentiality.'),q('Which is unethical?','Adding fake responses','Reporting honest results','Documenting procedures','Getting consent','Integrity.'),q('Which should student researchers NOT do?','Collect blood samples from classmates','Conduct anonymous surveys','Observe hand washing','Interview coaches with consent','Safety.'),q('Participants may:','Stop at any time without penalty','Never withdraw','Be required to finish','Be graded on answers','Voluntary.')],frq:{prompt:'Explain how your team would conduct ethical research, including informed consent, privacy and minimizing harm.',rubric:[['Principles',['belmont','respect for persons','beneficence','justice','irb']],['Consent',['informed consent','purpose','voluntary','stop','parental','assent','document']],['Privacy and harm',['anonymous','confidential','aggregate','secure','skip','resources','counselor']],['Integrity and permission',['permission','school','fabricate','falsify','honest','plagiarize']]],model:'Although formal IRB approval is not required, we would follow the Belmont principles: respect for persons, beneficence and justice. Before surveying students about sleep and stress, we would ask our school administration for permission and share our survey and consent statement with our advisor. Because most participants are minors, we would follow school policy, which may require parental permission along with each student’s own assent. Our consent statement would explain that our HOSA team is studying sleep and stress, that the anonymous survey takes about five minutes, that participation is voluntary and students may skip questions or stop at any time without penalty, how we will protect privacy and who to contact with questions, and continuing the survey would document consent. To protect privacy, we would not collect names or other identifying information, store responses in a password-protected file, report results only as group percentages and remove identifying details from any interview quotes, recording verbal consent for interviews in our notes. To minimize harm, we would avoid unnecessary personal questions, allow students to skip any question and include information about the school counselor and crisis resources, since stress can be a sensitive topic. We would select homerooms fairly across all grades for justice, never perform medical procedures and report our results honestly—even if they did not support our hypothesis—without fabricating, falsifying or plagiarizing, keeping our raw data and records through the competition.'},sources:[RULES,BELMONT]};
